it produced. That producer is unimplemented — the file's own closing paragraph says so, and DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md (dedff3e3…) only reaches it with the hedge 'including its applicability/no-finding fields where that SR applies'. No such record exists for this decomposition. What does exist is prose plus hat_refs cells (SR-184 TEST-ENGINEER/PRODUCT-FITNESS; SR-185 CONSISTENCY/MAINTAINER/TEST-ENGINEER; SR-186 MAINTAINER/PRODUCT-FITNESS) and the 'Opus disposition map' table; those are hand-authored prose and a per-row hat list, not per-perspective applicability decisions with explicit no-findings, and I do not present them as an execution of the absent producer. No machine-output pass is claimed or claimable. FIX: either (a) amend the procedure so the SR-161 read is explicitly conditional and require the inspection to RECORD the applicability decision ('SR-161 does not apply to this record because …'), reconciling it with the amendment record's 'where that SR applies' qualifier, or (b) hold TC-211's result open until the producer ships. Today the TC-211 result subsection must not record a full PASS; at most it can record the proportionality half plus this explicit gap.","THE CRITIQUE'S SUBJECT DIGEST DOES NOT BIND TO THE COUNTERPART RECORD I INSPECTED. docs/reviews/2026-09-06-oi85-record-closure-structured-opus.md (a377a2b3…) states 'Subject SHA256: fc1d9eac…', which is byte-identical to '# prompt-sha: fc1d9eac…' in the linked invocation log (073f702d…). That is a digest of the assembled PROMPT, not of docs/ai-template-redesign-2026-09-05-codex/DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md. The version of that file supplied to me is dedff3e3… and it contains material describing that same review's outcome ('The independent closure review returned APPROVE', 'The final authoring review's corrections are applied'), so it is demonstrably a LATER revision than whatever text the Critique judged. inspection-procedures.md instructs 'the inspector must identify the exact versions it reads' — I can do that for my own read, but no reader can establish that the Critique's positive semantic judgment (its G3 paragraph) was made against the H3 text now standing at dedff3e3…. FIX: have change-review records name the content digest of the reviewed artifact itself, not only the prompt sha.","THE LOAD-BEARING PREMISE OF THE H3 COUNTERPART DECISION IS STILL UNVERIFIED BY ANY REVIEW. The recorded justification for retaining LLR-035/SR-168/SR-070 and IF-164/LLR-198/SR-168 unchanged is that the change is 'descriptive-only' because IF-011's text-status mode was 'already-public'. DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md itself says 'The independent review must judge this semantic argument against the actual source and interface cells; a successful reference lookup is insufficient.' The Critique expressly declined the source half ('Source-level truth of \"already-public\" is outside my scope by the brief'), and this inspection has tools disabled and no source. The cell-level half checks out (LLR-035's detail already reads 'its status block via --status'; IF-011's data cell already reads '0 clean or vacuous · 1 invalid registry, stale HTML, or stale status snapshot under --check'; SR-070's AC carries the drift clause; IF-164 still names scripts/traj_status as owner). But the record's own required source check remains owed by someone with read access; until then the retain-the-counterpart decision rests on an assertion no review in this chain has tested.","INTERNAL CONTRADICTION IN THE POSITIVE RECORD'S PROVENANCE STATEMENT. The Critique states 'No provider session id was supplied by the invocation, so none is stated (G1's never-invented rule).' The invocation log it links in the same document carries '# session-id: a480da05-19ab-4d20-b6e8-396467f01a3d', repeated in the stream-json init event. G1 says metadata the reviewer cannot know 'is supplied by the existing invocation record, never invented' — which is exactly what happened here — so the correct sentence is 'the session id is carried by the linked invocation record', not 'none was supplied'. As written, a reader of the pair sees the record deny the existence of a fact its own linked evidence carries. The abstention was defensible at authoring time (the id is assigned by the launcher, not the prompt); the wording is not.","G5 IS THE ONE RUBRIC ANCHOR WITHOUT A QUOTABLE PARENT CLAUSE. docs/rubrics/change-review-records.md (81a0de90…) declares derivation from SN-024 (independent subjective acceptance), SN-037 (coordinated counterpart changes) and SN-012 (proportionate decomposition), realized by SR-184–SR-186. G1/G2 map cleanly onto SR-184's three duties and its TC-copy failure clause; G3 onto SR-185 including 'not discharged by reference-existence tests'; G4 onto SR-186's acceptance including required-tier retention and the no-quota constraint. G5 ('A contributor can follow the instructions as delivered') is a delivered-usability bar, and the Critique derives it from SR-186's 'shall require … and record' plus an SN-012 'abandonment rationale' that is nowhere quoted in the supplied records. Under SR-184's own bar — a rubric derived from the applicable SN/SR intent — each anchor should be traceable to a citable parent clause. Either quote G5's parent sentence in the rubric or fold it under G4. (Scope: SN-012's full cell was not supplied to me, so I judge this from the supplied documents' own citations, not from the need text.)"],"basis":"FRESH INSPECTOR IDENTITY AND WHAT THIS IS. Human-selected Opus5/high, assigned identity docs/reviews/2026-09-06-oi85-inspection-structured-opus.md, bound by the launcher to its fresh invocation log. I did not author the procedures, the amendments, the rubric, the SR/TC rows, or the prior Critique; I read that Critique as a record to inspect, never as authority or instruction. This is manual document inspection under TC-209/TC-210/TC-211. It executed no code, ran no test, and approves no artifact or Drafted row. Drafted statuses and unattended routing are unchanged; I propose no new metadata schema, form, count sensor or gate. Tools were disabled, so I could not write the three result subsections — they still read 'Not executed; reviewer/date/result pending', and the TC evidence anchors accordingly still point at pending results.\n\nRECORDS INSPECTED (path — SHA256 prefix as supplied): docs/test/inspection-procedures.md d8ccb16d…; docs/rubrics/change-review-records.md 81a0de90…; docs/reviews/2026-09-06-oi85-record-closure-structured-opus.md a377a2b3…; docs/ai-template-redesign-2026-09-05-codex/DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md dedff3e3…; docs/test/test-cases.toml bc2e10d4… (TC-209/210/211); docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml 758ecbd7… (SR-161/184/185/186/168/070); docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml 44e92fb1… (LLR-035, LLR-198); docs/requirements/interfaces.toml 00e0ac41… (IF-011, IF-164); docs/iteration/call_1603bfd28a3d4d3c9fa678845717bdf6-20260906-160650.log 073f702d….\n\n=== TC-209 — CRITIQUE ACCEPTANCE PROVENANCE INSPECTION: PASS ===\nPositive subject: the real Critique record a377a2b3… with its linked invocation log 073f702d…. Positive facts I ACTUALLY OBSERVED, not inferred:\n(1) FRESH NON-AUTHOR SESSION. The record declares an attended Critique on a human-chosen route with an assigned identity path the launcher binds to a fresh log, and states it authored none of the subject material. The linked log independently corroborates a distinct session: role ADVERSARIAL-REVIEW, model claude-opus-5, effort high, outcome COMPLETED, exit-code 0, started 2026-09-06T21:02:48Z / ended 21:06:50Z, session-id a480da05-19ab-4d20-b6e8-396467f01a3d, and a cache-read of only 835 tokens against 63,665 cache-create — consistent with a cold, non-continuing context rather than an authoring session resumed.\n(2) DIGEST BINDING TO THE INVOCATION. The record's 'Subject SHA256: fc1d9eac…' is character-identical to the log's '# prompt-sha: fc1d9eac…'. This is the one cross-document identity check available to me and it holds. (Its limitation is Finding 2.)\n(3) RUBRIC NAMED WITH A REVISION. The record names docs/rubrics/change-review-records.md and quotes a content digest matching the supplied rubric record (81a0de90…). The rubric itself carries the self-identifying rule 'A review names the Git revision or content digest of this rubric that it used' — satisfied.\n(4) INTENT SOURCES NAMED AND CHECKED BEFORE SCORING. SN-024, SN-037, SN-012 as needs; SR-184/185/186 as the Drafted realizations; SR-154, SR-162, SR-070, SR-168, SR-170, LLR-035/079/124/130, IF-011/IF-164 as adjacent Approved context. The record performs an explicit 'RUBRIC-AGAINST-INTENT CHECK, performed before scoring' mapping each anchor to its parent clause, and tests two named permissiveness risks (G1's deferral of unattended family heterogeneity to SR-154; G3's readability as satisfiable by naming any counterpart) rather than assuming the rubric adequate.\n(5) NUMBERED ANCHOR FOR EVERY VERDICT. All five anchors G1–G5 are judged individually with a stated PASS and reasoning; the record explicitly records coverage because the verdict has zero findings, which is what the rubric's closing line demands ('Cite the applicable numbered anchors even when the verdict has no findings'). Findings array is empty and no finding is left unanchored.\n(6) NO INVENTED METADATA. The record refuses to state a provider session id it believed unsupplied and declares its unread scope (no test run, no P9R source diff, no registry beyond excerpts, no TC/LLR text). That is the correct direction of error under G1, though its wording is Finding 4.\n(7) COPIED-RUBRIC CHECK, RE-DRIVEN BY ME. The Critique's basis for 'the rubric is not TC-derived' was weak in kind — it argued from absence ('no TC text exists in the brief'), i.e. lack of opportunity rather than positive derivation. I could do better: TC-209/TC-210/TC-211 method and expected strings WERE supplied to me (bc2e10d4…), and I compared them against G1–G5. The rubric is not a copy: its anchors are stated as reconstructible-decision criteria grounded in named SN clauses, while the TC methods are procedural pointers to inspection-procedures.md anchors; wording, structure and grounding differ. SR-184's TC-copy failure clause is therefore not triggered by the real rubric.\nABNORMAL SAMPLE — 'Missing provenance': REJECTED, four separate Inspection findings, one per omitted required fact (reviewer/session not recorded; rubric not named; no SN/SR intent basis; no numbered anchor citations), all in class B1 for identity/provenance and B2 for unanchored acceptance. An APPROVE verdict standing on none of the four is exactly the record SR-184's acceptance says fails; the fact that the verdict word is well-formed is irrelevant.\nABNORMAL SAMPLE — 'Copied rubric': REJECTED DESPITE EVERY PROVENANCE SLOT BEING POPULATED. fixture-reviewer, fixture-session, verdict APPROVE, anchor G1, rubric 'fixture-rubric revision 1', intent sources SN-024 and SR-184 — four-for-four on the fields the first abnormal omits. It still fails, because the record itself declares the rubric was copied verbatim from the verifying TC-209 method 'without reading or independently deriving from those parents'. SR-184's acceptance criteria make that an independent failure mode ('a record whose rubric is copied from the verifying TC without independent derivation from the SN/SR intent fails'), and rubric G2/B2 name it directly: 'A populated provenance field alone cannot make that copied bar adequate.' Naming SN-024 and SR-184 is here a citation of parents the record admits it never read — nominal provenance, not derivation. Two aggravating observations: the fixture cites a single anchor G1 for a whole verdict while declaring a rubric ('fixture-rubric revision 1') whose anchor namespace is not the real rubric's, so the anchor id is unresolvable; and a rubric copied from the very TC that verifies it is circular by construction — the test can never be more demanding than the artifact it grades. This is the discriminating case for the procedure and it discriminates correctly.\nTC-209 VERDICT: PASS. The complete record is accepted with independently derived provenance; both abnormal records are rejected, including the fully populated one.\n\n=== TC-210 — REQUIREMENT/INTERFACE COUNTERPART REVIEW INSPECTION: PASS ===\nPositive subject: the H3 material in DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md dedff3e3… ('Implementation review subject (2026-09-06)') plus the Critique's G3 judgment. Positive facts ACTUALLY OBSERVED:\n(1) CHANGED SIDE NAMED: the LLR source pointers for the rendering implementation, and IF-011's description completed for its text-status mode.\n(2) AFFECTED COUNTERPARTS NAMED, both chains, at the requirement/interface tier: IF-011 → LLR-035 → SR-168 and SR-070; paired status surface IF-164 → LLR-198 → SR-168.\n(3) EXPLICIT RETAIN JUSTIFICATION CARRIED, not merely asserted: 'The decision is descriptive-only: the SR-168/SR-070 asserted behavior remains unchanged while the interface description records the existing status mode. The command remains gen_trajectory.py, its HTML and status checks retain exit 0/1, and IF-164 still names traj_status as the status writer. Retaining those counterparts preserves the supported calling convention; moving the private HTML implementation is not a reason to change a consumer's command.'\n(4) AN INDEPENDENT SEMANTIC DECISION IS RECORDED, and I re-drove its checkable half against the supplied cells rather than accepting it: LLR-035's detail does already read 'its status block via --status' (44e92fb1…), so the interface text records behavior an Approved row already asserts; IF-011's data cell does read '0 clean or vacuous · 1 invalid registry, stale HTML, or stale status snapshot under --check' (00e0ac41…); SR-070's acceptance does carry 'a committed copy that has drifted from its sources fails its freshness contract while a current one passes' (758ecbd7…), which is the requirement that exit code realizes; IF-164's owner cell is 'scripts/traj_status' and LLR-198's module cell includes traj_status.py with a documented re-export shim keeping the generated surfaces byte-identical — so 'IF-164 still names traj_status as the status writer' verifies. The Critique also records a MINOR it considered and rejected (not enumerating LLR-130/LLR-079), which is the shape of a decision rather than a citation. Two cited rows, LLR-130 and LLR-124, were not supplied to me; I could not verify those two and did not rely on them.\nABNORMAL SAMPLE — 'Reference-only counterpart': REJECTED. The change asserts IF-011's stale result is now exit 2 while LLR-035/SR-168/SR-070 and the callers retain existing behavior, and its review says only that all named IDs and endpoints resolve and therefore it passes. Finding, class B3 / rubric G3: a one-sided change with no defensible counterpart decision — it records neither a corresponding behavioral change nor a reason retaining the counterparts preserves meaning. Per SR-185's acceptance, 'the independent reviewer's semantic decision is recorded and is not discharged by reference-existence tests', and per the procedure, 'A reference-existence result alone cannot discharge this procedure.' The concrete harm is visible in the live cells and is exactly what a resolver cannot see: IF-011's registered consumer is scripts/check, and the live contract routes stale-HTML/stale-status to exit 1; moving the stale result to exit 2 while the consumer and SR-070's freshness AC keep treating 1 as the stale signal means a drifted committed copy could stop failing its freshness contract. Every id in the change still resolves while the promise at the boundary silently breaks — the discriminating property this TC exists to catch. Rejected correctly.\nTC-210 VERDICT: PASS, within the scope limit recorded as Finding 3 (the 'already-public' premise remains unverified against source by any review in this chain).\n\n=== TC-211 — DECOMPOSITION PROPORTIONALITY INSPECTION: INCOMPLETE (negative half PASS, positive half not dischargeable) ===\nPOSITIVE FACTS ACTUALLY OBSERVED on the independent-value half:\n(1) INDEPENDENT CHILD PURPOSE IS REAL, verified against the registries rather than taken from the record's summary. The three children answer three different acceptance questions under three DIFFERENT need parents — 758ecbd7… shows sn_refs [\"SN-024\"] for SR-184, [\"SN-037\"] for SR-185, [\"SN-012\"] for SR-186 — and their requirement sentences are materially distinct obligations (acceptance-record provenance; counterpart change/justification at a seam; each additional child carrying independent purpose plus a recorded stopping decision). None paraphrases another. Their hat_refs differ, and SR-186 alone carries aspect='process', which the record explains as a deliberate non-inflation of the schema for the other two.\n(2) INDEPENDENT VERIFICATION PURPOSE IS REAL, not mirrored. bc2e10d4… shows each TC verifying a different SR, naming a different procedure anchor, and asserting a different expected judgment: TC-209 discriminates independently derived provenance from missing fields AND from a populated-but-TC-copied rubric; TC-210 discriminates semantic agreement from valid references; TC-211 discriminates a purposeful child from a paraphrasing one while preserving required tiers. No TC replays another's assertion.\n(3) THE STOPPING RULE IS ACTUALLY STATED, at the independent-value boundary and with a reason: 'Because SR-186 selects Verification=Inspection, its selected verification method permits this direct SR→TC link while retaining every tier required by that method; another LLR would duplicate these review judgments without defining a separate mechanism. Further splitting is not justified by another label for the same question.' The parallel statement for SR-184 is 'eligible for a direct TC procedure without inventing an LLR/TC mechanism pair', with LLR-048/TC-048 and LLR-076 left under SR-154 rather than re-homed, and mechanical evidence left with SR-154/SR-162. That is a stop justified by absence of a distinct mechanism, which is the correct criterion, not by a count.\n(4) THE REQUIRED SPINE REMAINS THE SCOPE BOUNDARY. SR-186's rationale states it 'never permits omitting a required SN→SR→LLR→TC tier or replacing real verification with a count' and imposes 'no row-count cap, deletion quota, or new machine gate'; its acceptance is scoped to 'each additional child within a required tier'. The record cites PROCESS.md:482-487 and 737-744 for the LLR-exemption; PROCESS.md was NOT supplied to me, so I record those two citations as unverified (scope limit) and note the exemption is at least internally consistent with the three rows' Verification=Inspection cells.\nABNORMAL SAMPLE — 'Paraphrasing child': REJECTED. The unminted candidate under SR-186→TC-211 reading 'the process shall keep decomposition proportionate' is a restatement of its parent's own decision (SR-186 already requires each additional child to carry independent purpose and the stopping decision to be recorded), and it is 'verified by the same Inspection judgment without another decision or case' — so it adds neither an independent decision nor an independent verification. Its declared purpose, 'to make the chain look more detailed', is precisely the row-count motive SR-186's rationale forbids and rubric G4/B4 name ('paraphrases, mirrored tests and a row-count target do not earn extra rows'). Finding, class B4. Note it sits INSIDE a required tier under an SR that is legitimately LLR-exempt, so it is pure review and maintenance cost with no verification gained. Rejected correctly, and rejecting it does not omit a required tier: TC-211 already supplies SR-186's mandatory TC.\nSR-161 MACHINE PERSPECTIVE-RECORD OUTPUT — SEPARATE EXPLANATION AS REQUIRED. YES, IT MAKES THIS SAMPLE INCOMPLETE. The procedure's positive half has two required inputs and only one exists. The SR-161 producer is an existing, unimplemented obligation, so there is no machine-readable perspective record carrying each declared perspective's applicability decision and its requirements-or-explicit-no-finding for this decomposition. I did not substitute the hat_refs cells or the prose 'Opus disposition map' for it, and I claim no machine-output pass: a prose worksheet is not an execution of an absent producer, and presenting one as such would be the exact misreporting the procedure warns against. I therefore record TC-211 as INCOMPLETE rather than PASS — its proportionality reasoning is demonstrated and its counterexample is correctly rejected, but the procedure as written cannot be discharged today. Finding 1 states the two acceptable repairs. I invented no substitute evidence.\n\nWHY CHANGES_REQUESTED. The instruction is APPROVE only if all three procedures' expected judgments are demonstrated. TC-209 and TC-210 are demonstrated in full, including both discriminating negatives. TC-211's negative is demonstrated but its positive cannot be, for a reason internal to the procedure and its unimplemented SR-161 dependency. The five findings are residual defects in the real records — the SR-161 sample gap, the prompt-sha-vs-artifact version binding, the unverified 'already-public' source premise, the session-id contradiction, and G5's underived anchor. The four planted abnormal samples were detected as designed and are reported above as procedure results, not as findings, since their detection is the expected outcome rather than a defect.\n\nSCOPE LIMITS. Tools disabled; I judged only the supplied text and could not open the repository, run any check, or record the three result subsections. NOT SUPPLIED and therefore not verified by me: project-trajectory/PROCESS.md (so the :482-487 and :737-744 tier citations are unverified), PROCESS_OPTIONS.md, the critique and reviewer prompt templates, the spine-authoring skill, the P9R source diff and any script source, LLR-130, LLR-124, LLR-079, LLR-048, LLR-076, LLR-187, TC-182, SR-154, SR-155, SR-162, SR-170, SR-175, SR-183, and the full SN-007/SN-012/SN-024/SN-026/SN-036/SN-037 cells. Consequently every quotation the prior Critique makes from those files is unverified by me; TC-209 is unaffected because it checks the record's process and provenance, not the truth of the artifact claims, and TC-210/TC-211 are judged from the record's own recorded decisions plus the registry cells that were supplied. I judged no artifact quality, approved no Drafted row, changed no status or routing, and added no schema."},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"2bb924ef-2e4c-4f6d-ae30-eea99526b345"}
